00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:02.950 align:start position:0%
 
now we can go to uh wigan and speak to  our next guest who is the  shadow foreign secretary lisa nandy so  much to talk to you about uh when it  comes to global affairs but  i just want to start off with uh  coronavirus if i may uh the prime  minister  uh giving an interview to the sunday  telegraph today where he has said a  national lockdown is like a nuclear  deterrent  he doesn't want to have to use it do you  think that boris johnson is right to be  so reluctant  to consider implementing another  national lockdown  well it's right to implement local or  national lockdowns if they're needed but  i think he's right  to aspire to open up the economy again  and get things  moving the trouble is that just saying  it doesn't make it  so and what we actually need is a plan  there are great  gaping holes in the plan that the prime  minister announced  on friday you only have to go down to  the local high street to see what the  problem is  some of the shops and restaurants have  now opened up pubs as well  but many people aren't coming out of  their houses they aren't spending again  in the economy  because they're nervous about what this  means whether there's going to be a  second wave whether the nhs is going to  be overwhelmed  and we really do need to get to grips  with the test  trace and isolate system which the  government accepts is not  still fully functional matt hancock  yesterday  said last night suggested that he might  now start giving  information to public health directors  about positive tests that is long  overdue  and very welcome but we also need to see  tests back within  24 hours we need to see far more  contacts traced and told to isolate far  more quickly  because the government is actually going  backwards on that and we need a mass  winter flu vaccination program as well  there was nothing about that in the  government's plans on friday and unless  people feel confident  that there are plans for a second wave  that is going to be avoided that the nhs  isn't going to be overwhelmed i just  don't think that the economy will get  back up and running  as quickly as the prime minister hopes  okay  now just to move on to china you've  taken a pretty hard-line stance on the  uk's relationship  with china saying that there's been a  naivety about it over the last decade  but of course china is a very important  trading partner to us  particularly at a time when we're  dealing with the economic hits of  coronavirus  leaving the european union the sunday  times today reporting that the chinese  firm tick-tock no longer basing itself  in the uk there's thousands of jobs tied  up there  how concerned are you and how much  should we factor in when thinking about  our relationship with china  that's all important economic  relationship  well we've got to be in a position first  and foremost to safeguard our national  security  and whilst chinese investment is very  welcome  in the uk there are serious concerns  which i've been raising  actually for four years now i was the  shadow energy secretary who raised  concerns about the hinckley point  nuclear power station  that we shouldn't be handing over large  chunks of our  key infrastructure to chinese  government-backed firms here in the uk  and that's what has prompted the route  about huawei for several years now we've  been saying to the government you this  is a high-risk vendor three consecutive  conservative prime ministers have been  told by their own security services that  this is a high-risk vendor  and yet have done nothing to try and  reduce the reliance on this company  in our 5g network it doesn't help  britain's economic prosperity  to have failed to safeguard national  security that's got to be  the plank on which we base everything  else and the truth is that in relation  to huawei  if we'd acted sooner if the government  had paid heed to the warnings not just  from labor but from conservative  backbenchers  as well and from their own security  services then we wouldn't be in a  position now where the rollout of 5g  is delayed and where the costs have  increased because we just didn't have a  plan  okay important of course i said the  government has obviously as you as you  say  reversed its position on huawei um how  concerned are you by some of the human  rights issues in  china i'm particularly thinking about  the treatment of the uyghurs the muslim  minority community in detention camps  allegations that women are being forced  to be sterilized  how should the uk be responding  well we uh we're appalled by what has is  emerging out of the xinjiang  province in relation to the uyghurs and  also very concerned about the pattern of  behavior that we're seeing  develop from the chinese government i  should say our quarrel is not with the  chinese people that relationship is  long deep and important and those  cultural exchanges medical and  scientific  research collaborations are incredibly  important to britain  and to china and to the rest of the  world but we cannot stand by  while we see a pattern of behavior where  freedom democracy  the rule of law and human rights are  abused  and do nothing about it at all now the  government has  started to take steps to address this in  the last few weeks  we've seen a gesture made to the bno  passport holders in hong kong  which has had a very very tough reaction  from the chinese government i think  proving why  i have been saying on others for some  time that we were right to say that we  need a strategy to deal with china  across government the truth is that for  a decade now we've had no strategy at  home no strategy abroad we haven't been  building  our own strategic independence in  relation to things like 5g  and nuclear so that we've got homegrown  alternatives and aren't overly reliant  on china we haven't been building  alliances with other democratic nations  to make sure  that we're not squeezed by china on a  world  stage um we haven't got the strategic  independence that we need and that  absolutely has to change there is also  one very concrete thing that the  government could do  in relation to helping the uyghur  people at the moment and that is to  freeze the assets of any of the chinese  officials  that are involved in those human rights  abuses over in china we've got new  legislation now we've been pushing the  government  to do that for two years the uk should  not be a haven  for people who abuse human rights  overseas  okay another country of course that we  need a strategic  uh relationship with or a strategic  ambition with is  russia uh allegations this week that  russia is behind  online attempts to steal information on  the uk's coronavirus  vaccine now you've accused conservatives  of being  naive in their relationship to china  previously  do you think the labour party has been  naive in its  relationship with russia  well i think we got it wrong on russia  and i made a feature of this during the  labour leadership campaign because i  felt that it really needed to be said  when the salisbury attacks happened we  prevaricated we equivocated we  called for dialogue at a moment when  chemical weapons had been used on the  streets of the uk  and what that did was not only to let an  authoritarian regime that has invaded  its neighbours that has interfered  in elections across european democratic  countries  over several years that has had an  appalling record of human rights against  its own people against lgbt people  muslims and other minorities  and use chemical weapons in the streets  of the uk we didn't just let them off  the hook we also  let the conservative government off the  hook because  i believe the conservatives have been  desperately slow to wake up  to the threat that is posed by the  current  putin administration and we should have  been much quicker  to act in relation to that and by  prevaricating about  issues like salisbury we let the tories  off the hook  i have i strongly believe that that has  to change  and that we have to have a much more  strategic approach  to russia but at the current time what  we've got is a government that on the  one hand  in the foreign office is saying that  they're going to take strong steps  to freeze the assets of russian  officials who are involved in the murder  of sergey  magnitsky and others on the other hand  we've got another government department  uk export finance  which is underwriting to a huge extent  backed by the british taxpayer the  activities of putin's state-backed  oil and gas company gazprom it is the  biggest beneficiary of uk  export finance currently at the moment  this is a very very confused approach  from the uk government and we've got to  have  a review of that finance we've got to  have a more strategic approach in  relation to russia  or we will pay the price pretty clear uh  criticism there are both  uh the previous labour and conservative  response to  russia now it feels like we're rushing  through so many topics here but there  are so much to get through when it comes  to  world affairs with you so i wonder if  you'll forgive me for that  now this week a court ruled that shamima  begum who of course is the bethnal green  schoolgirl who left the united kingdom  to join islamic state in  syria has been granted the right to  return to the united kingdom  to contest the fact that the government  stripped her of her british  citizenship now both you and kia stammer  have previously said  that she's entitled to come back to  britain do you think that schuman bagan  should come back to britain well  we've i think those words were proven  right by the court judgment  that we saw handed down in the last few  days  um i don't welcome the prospect of not  just shamima bagan but  many people coming back to britain who  may be a risk  to us um you know we've got a history in  the uk  in the northwest here of terrorist  incidents just down the road  uh the family of lee rigby are in berry  we had a  ira attack in warrington um when i was a  child which we still remember  every year it's heartbreaking and the  impact is enormous so  i don't welcome the prospect of many  people now being  able to come back to britain who may  pose a risk to us and the security  services say that they do  but the home secretary has made a real  mess of this there were two ways to  handle it  one would have been to take legal advice  and see if there was a basis for denying  not just shamima bagan but many other  people  who wanted to appeal access to britain  whether there was a way to give them a  fair trial  overseas the other would have been to  bring them back  to britain in order to face justice and  what happened  was neither we had a home secretary who  shot from the hip  who tried to play to the gallery and as  a consequence we're now  we now face a real problem shamima begum  is coming back  to have a trial uh she has a right to a  fair trial the courts have  have determined that but it sets a  precedent so many other people  may now return from overseas and it's  not at all clear that the uk  is prepared for that we could have  avoided this mess if the home secretary  had just taken proper legal advice  observed the rule of law and handled  this process properly  in the first place at the same though at  the same time though um  because we are uh haven't got too much  time left in the interview but some  people  listening to you will think that  actually why is labour effectively  siding with shamima bagan someone who  they would feel gave up her right to  british citizenship  when she made the decision to join  islamic state  well i don't think that is about siding  with somebody who  joined islamic state and sought to harm  people in  britain i think that is about saying  that people who take those  actions must face justice  and there were two ways that could have  happened the home secretary could have  taken  proper legal advice to see if there was  a basis to deny her entry into the uk  and dealt with it that way or he said  javid at the time could have brought  shamima bagan back to the uk  to face justice through the british  justice system but he didn't either  and as a consequence this case has been  dragging on for years and we're now in a  position where  it is it's clear that shamima begum is  returning to the uk but it's not at all  clear that we're prepared for that  or for the precedent that this  potentially sets we should have just  handled this properly  in order to make sure that justice was  done  and we should have been thinking about a  strategy to make sure that  that this doesn't keep repeatedly  happening you know one of the things  that  that was said at the time was that a 15  year old  from bethnal green travelling uh making  that  disastrous choice to travel overseas in  order to join  isis fighters um this has happened too  often  okay thank you thank you uh for that now  later on in the programme another  important story that i'm keen to talk to  you about and later on the program  we're going to be talking to the mother  of harry dunn who of course is the  british teenager who was killed in a  road traffic accident  the other person involved and the  crewless then fled to the united states  where she claimed diplomatic immunity  now they want the case raised with the  u.s secretary of state mike pompeo when  he's in  the uk next week what practically  can the british government do about this  awful case or is there nothing they can  more that they can do  well i think the first thing that would  really help the family who i've  met with and admire enormously for their  for their bravery and their stoicism  through all of this is to be honest  about what happened there were real  inconsistencies in the account that the  foreign secretary has given  about how ann sakulas came to leave the  united kingdom  it's starting to look likely thanks to  the work of sky news and others  who've been investigating this that she  was given the green light to leave by  the united kingdom  wrongly it appears and the family wasn't  informed so the family need honesty they  deserve that but they also need  a trial they need a verdict um they need  justice for harry  and when kirsten and i met with the u.s  ambassador to the uk we raised this case  i think there is a willingness on the  part of the u.s authorities  to actually try to resolve this but we  need  the uk government to to step up and  approach this with a level of honesty  and transparency that has been  completely  lacking so far it's disrespectful to  harry but it also  sends a warning signal to a lot of  people around the uk  because if this happened to your child i  mean i would want to know  that the british government was on my  side that it wasn't secretly working  with other countries  in order to let the person who did it  off the hook  this family deserves justice and we've  got to make sure that they get it  okay and of course we'll be hearing the  government's response to that with  dominic raab the foreign secretary later  on in the programme as well

